Well, I guess this is a matter of opinion.  I run on a 386/25 with
a 4MB disk cache and a *VERY* fast 18ms hard disk.  Since I run in
the DOS environment (thus I use the overlayed version), I have to
put up with it constantly accessing the disk.  This is OK (as soon
as the cache is full of it things get a lot faster), but it still
seems a little odd that thing are this slow on this fast of a 
machine.  Now before someone goes off and begins using their
blessed bullwhipe +25 on me in defense of the devlopment team, I don't
want this comment to be construed as an attack on them (I have
nothing but the highest respect for the incredible job they have
done, I don't have the slightest idea how the overlay system works
and Im impressed that it does).  I simply think it is slower than I
expected.  (What DID I expect?  Well, hmmm, I guess I expected it to
be faster than UNIX versions because the PC doesn't have to run
UNIX and is a single-user single-task machine) 

In comparison with a couple of versions I've played on various
UNIX machines, I think it is a little slower, but not much.  As to
consider it a DOG, well....It depends on the machine.  If I were
running on a 10MHz system, I think it might be a dog.  But, hey,
you can play it at all, can't you?  I think this is such an improvment
over not having it at all, that I am glad to wait 1s for
the system to catch up to me...

One thought though, what about allowing confirmation of attacks on ALL
monsters be a compile directive or a run-time option.  That way, the
people with machines that run quickly don't have to have it, but
those with slow PCs can.  This would probably be easier to code than
selectively coding particular monsters that way.

(How's that for riding the middle of the fence...)

